      "math_academy_id": "q-164267",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0).$",
